-- title:
--   Counting $k$-th roots in the units of $\mathbb{Z}/2^{n+2}$
-- statement:
--   **The number of $k$-th roots in $(\mathbb{Z}/2^{n+2})^{\times}$.**
--
--   For $m = 2^{n+2}$ with $n \ge 0$ and any unit $a$,
--
--   $$\#\{x \in (\mathbb{Z}/2^{n+2})^{\times} : x^{k} = a\}
--   \;\le\; \gcd(k,2)\cdot\gcd(k,2^{n}).$$
--
--   The group $(\mathbb{Z}/2^{n+2})^{\times}$ is the one classical exception to the cyclicity of
--   $(\mathbb{Z}/p^{e})^{\times}$: for $2^{e}$ with $e \ge 3$ it is **not** cyclic but splits as
--
--   $$(\mathbb{Z}/2^{n+2})^{\times} \;\cong\; \mathbb{Z}/2 \;\times\; \mathbb{Z}/2^{n},$$
--
--   generated by $-1$ and by $3$ (or $5$). The bound is the product of the cyclic-factor counts:
--   each factor $\mathbb{Z}/d$ contributes at most $\gcd(k,d)$ solutions, giving
--   $\gcd(k,2)\gcd(k,2^{n})$.
--
--   This is the case one must treat separately in any statement about $k$-th power residues modulo
--   a general integer, since the Chinese remainder theorem reduces the problem to prime powers and
--   the prime $2$ behaves differently from the odd primes.
--
--   **Formalization note.** The exponent is written $2^{n+2}$ so that $n \ge 0$ covers exactly the
--   moduli $\ge 4$, where the two-factor decomposition applies.
-- source:
--   Classical; see Ireland & Rosen, *A Classical Introduction to Modern Number Theory*, §4.1, and Hardy & Wright, §6.10. Lean proof extracted from `Salt/Maynard/PpRootTwo.lean` of the Salt project, https://github.com/jyh/salt (Apache-2.0, Jason Hickey).

import Mathlib

namespace CyclicGroups

theorem card_pow_eq_units_two_pow_le {n : ℕ} (k : ℕ) (a : (ZMod (2 ^ (n + 2)))ˣ) :
    (Finset.univ.filter (fun x : (ZMod (2 ^ (n + 2)))ˣ => x ^ k = a)).card
      ≤ Nat.gcd k 2 * Nat.gcd k (2 ^ n) := by sorry

end CyclicGroups
